    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your frustration, and I apologize for the excessive goblin metaphors in my previous response. You are absolutely right to want the technical explanation without the distractions.\n\nThe delay occurs due to **DNS caching** and **Time To Live (TTL)** values. When a DNS resolver (like Google's or Cloudflare's) fetches your A record, it stores the result in its local cache for a duration specified by the TTL field in the DNS response. This TTL is essentially a \"how long to keep this info before checking again\" timer.\n\nIf your client's previous TTL was set to a high value (e.g., 86400 seconds or 24 hours), the resolvers will continue serving the old IP address to users until their cached records expire and they perform a fresh query. Once the TTL expires, the resolvers fetch the updated record from your authoritative nameservers, and the new IP begins propagating. To speed this up, you can lower the TTL before making changes, though this means accepting that the old IP will be served for a shorter period immediately after the change.",
