    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, lowering the TTL well in advance is the standard best practice to ensure a smoother migration, though it does have a few other side effects beyond just propagation speed.\n\nWhen you lower the TTL, you are essentially telling the internet's caching system, \"Hey, I'm changing my mind often, so please check my records more frequently.\" This means that if a user queries your domain *after* you've lowered the TTL but *before* you've actually changed the A record, they will get the old IP address for a very short time (perhaps just a few minutes instead of hours or days). This is actually beneficial during a migration because it allows you to switch the A record to the new IP and have the change propagate almost instantly for everyone who hasn't cached the old record yet.\n\nHowever, there are a couple of other things to consider:\n\n1.  **Increased Network Load:** With a lower TTL, DNS resolvers have to query your authoritative nameservers much more frequently to verify if the records have changed. This generates more traffic on your DNS server and across the internet's resolver network. If you have a very high-traffic site, this could theoretically increase the load on your DNS infrastructure, though for most standard setups,",
